<?php

return [
    ['A newborn hemolytic disease workup finds a moderately severe anemia in an A-positive infant born to an A-negative mother.', 'The major antibody responsible for classic ABO hemolytic disease of the newborn is generally which class of antibody?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'IgA defined and capsular', 'IgG anti-A or anti-B antibodies from a group O mother', 'IgM anti-D', 'IgE antiprojic', 'Severe ABO HDN occurs when a group O mother with IgG class anti-A or anti-B antibodies carries a non-O infant; the maternal IgG crosses the placenta and coats the fetal red cells identified by the direct antiglobulin test. IgM antibodies do not cross the placenta and anti-D is the Rh hemolytic issue rather than the ABO one.'],
    ['A blood bank performs forward and reverse ABO typing, and the technologist reviews the results for a patient with an AB discrepancy.', 'The reverse (serum) typing of the ABO system matches which antibody of the patient?', 'easy', 'C', 'Antibody to A2', 'Antibody to the ABO antigen', 'Antibody to the A or B antigen absent from the patients own cells', 'Antibody to H substance', 'Reverse typing tests the patient serum for anti-A and anti-B, which are naturally occurring IgM antibodies directed against the A or B antigens the patient does not carry. The pattern confirms the forward typing by cross-checking the red cell type and plasma antibodies.'],
    ['An ABO discrepancy is being worked up in a blood bank:', 'The most likely cause of an AB discrepancy in a newborn, and in an older adult after a bone marrow transplant, is respectively which pair?', 'advanced', 'D', 'Cold aggregates and enzyme excess', 'Weak reaction from factor VIII and an acquired cold antibody', 'Pregnant state anti-B and albumin', 'Weak ABO antigens in the newborn; and a changed red cell type after donor engraftment', 'ABO discrepancies from weak antigen expression appear in newborns; after an ABO-mismatched transplant the red cell type may convert to the donor type, and mixed-field forward typing results. Both situations produce forward-reverse mismatches that need investigation rather than reflex.'],
    ['A technologist resolves a forward-reverse ABO discrepancy caused by the Bombay phenotype.', 'A patient with the Bombay (Oh) phenotype has which characteristic red cell and serum type?', 'advanced', 'A', 'No A, B, or H antigen on the cells with anti-H, anti-A, and anti-B in the serum', 'Strong H antigen with weak A', 'Normal H with anti-A', 'Missing only anti-B', 'The Bombay phenotype lacks the H antigen, so the red cells have no A, B, or H and the serum contains anti-H along with anti-A and anti-B; transfusion requires H-negative units only. A and H relate by separate fucosyl transferase, so the absence of H takes away the substrate for the A and B transferases.'],
    ['A group A patient is typed; the forward reaction is strongly positive with anti-A and weakly positive with anti-A1 lectin testing.', 'A strong anti-A agglutination with a weak anti-A1 lectin reaction identifies which ABO subgroup?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'The A1 subgroup', 'The A2 subgroup', 'The A3 subgroup', 'The weak Ax subgroup', 'A2 red cells react strongly with anti-A but weakly or not at all with the anti-A1 lectin (Dolichos biflorus); A2B patients may also form anti-A1 in serum. The A1 subgroup reacts strongly with both, and A3 and Ax are rarer subgroups with distinct reaction strengths.'],
    ['A pregnant patient is typed as group O, RhD positive, but the typing shows weak D reactivity on the immediate spin and needs a more accurate determination.', 'Weak Du (weak D) red cells are best confirmed by which additional step?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'Repeat forward only', 'Read at 4 C', 'Incubation at 37 C with anti-D and an antiglobulin phase', 'Suspension in saline gel', 'Weak D testing exposes the red cells to anti-D, incubates at 37 C, and adds the antiglobulin (IAT) phase to detect the small number of D sites; positive IAT results designate the unit as D positive. Repeat and saline methods would not reliably separate weak D from negative.'],
    ['An RhD-negative patient with a history develops anti-D; the patient is Rh positive on a retype using a polyclonal anti-D reagent.', 'The antigenic difference that allows a weak D or partial D variant to look D negative with some reagents involves what property?', 'advanced', 'D', 'Negative for C antigen', 'Absence of the entire rhs', 'A missing fucose epitope', 'A quantitative or qualitative D variation where some epitopes are absent and others are weak', 'Partial D variants lack part of the D epitope mosaic, while weak D has reduced site numbers; standard monoclonal anti-D may not detect some variants, so an enhanced IAT method or molecular typing resolves the status. The C antigen and fucose do not describe the D mosaic change.'],
    ['A patient has a negative antibody screen; a crossmatch is performed using the immediate spin and turns out compatible.', 'An immediate spin crossmatch is appropriate only when which conditions are met?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Negative antibody screen in a patient with no history of clinically significant antibodies and ABO-compatible units', 'Positive screen with no clinical need', 'Whenever a warm antibody is present', 'As an initial screening of all patients', 'Immediate spin crossmatch detects only ABO incompatibility; it is valid when the antibody screen is negative and the patient has no history of clinically significant antibodies. With a positive screen, an antiglobulin (AHG) crossmatch or electronic crossmatch with documented historical negatives is required.'],
    ['A patient with a positive antibody screen needs an indirect antiglobulin test to identify the antibody.', 'The antiglobulin technique in the indirect antiglobulin test detects antibody that has bound to the red cells during which phase?', 'easy', 'B', 'Immediate spin phase', '37 C incubation phase', 'Cold room phase', 'Platelet phase', 'In the IAT, the serum is incubated with reagent red cells at 37 C so antibody attaches, then anti-human globulin detects the bound globulin; the pattern of reactions across the panel identifies the specificity. Immediate and cold phases do not coat reagent cells in the intended sensitizing step.'],
    ['A positive antibody screen is worked up with a panel of reagent red cells; one antibody of the Duffy system is suspected.', 'An antibody that reacts with the Fy(a) and Fy(b) antigens and does not react with Duffy-negative cells is detected by which screening method?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'Saline at 4 C', 'Enzyme treatment', 'Indirect antiglobulin using cells heterozygous for Fy antigens', 'Immediate spin only', 'Duffy antibodies (anti-Fy-a and anti-Fy-b) are detected by IAT with appropriate heterozygous cells; they are clinically significant and cause delayed hemolytic reactions. Saline cold or immediate spin testing cannot pick them up, and enzyme treatment destroys the Duffy antigens, so enzyme panels are not used for this identification.'],
    ['A patient with a history of prior transfusion has a delayed hemolytic reaction; the antibody is a Kidd antibody.', 'Kidd antibodies are notorious for which clinical and laboratory behavior?', 'advanced', 'B', 'They never cause reactions', 'They drop to undetectable levels between transfusions and can cause delayed hemolytic reactions on re-exposure', 'They are always IgM', 'They attack H antigen', 'Anti-Jk-a and anti-Jk-b are laboratory evanescent: the antibody titer falls below the detection limit, yet an anamnestic response on re-exposure causes severe delayed hemolysis and high-titer acute hemolysis; complement fixing arises because Kidd antibodies bind complement. The evanescence is the classic pitfall of this specificity.'],
    ['An antibody identification panel shows reactivity in the AHG phase with all cells tested except the rr cells; the history includes multiple pregnancies.', 'An antibody showing the dosage reactions and the neutralization pattern consistent with anti-c or anti-e is evaluated with which additional cells?', 'advanced', 'D', 'Cells without Duffy', 'Cells with only Jk antigens', 'Enzyme treated cells only', 'Selected typed cells to confirm the corresponding antigen dosage pattern', 'Antibodies to c and e show dosage: they react stronger with homozygous cells and weaker with heterozygous cells, so a panel with typed cells (and if needed ZZAP or enzyme treatment) confirms the specificity predicted by the pattern. The neutralization tests for Lewis and other saline antibody specificities do not apply to these common Rh antibodies.'],
    ['A patient with a positive DAT has an autoimmune hemolytic anemia; the eluate and serum studies show a panagglutinin.', 'A panagglutinin reacting with all reagent cells at the AHG phase is most consistent with which antibody type?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'IgM cold', 'Warm autoantibody', 'The anti-A alloantibody', 'Heterophile antibody', 'Warm autoantibodies may react with all cells in the panel, so autoadsorption and cell removal (e.g., ZZAP) are used to identify the underlying clinically significant alloantibody. Cold agglutinins and anti-A show distinctive specificity, and heterophile antibodies are not red cell autoantibodies.'],
    ['A patient is being crossmatched for an elective surgery; their antibody screen is negative on a Manually generated electronic file.', 'The electronic crossmatch can safely issue blood when which combination of requirements is met?', 'advanced', 'A', 'Two ABO/Rh typings on file, a negative current antibody screen, and computer verification of ABO compatibility', 'A positive antibody screen with a negative control', 'An only specimen with no history', 'Any patient with a negative DAT', 'Electronic (computer) crossmatch requires a current ABO/Rh type determined twice, a negative antibody screen (including history), and computer logic that prevents release of ABO-incompatible units; it removes the immediate spin step. Positive screens and unverified records exclude the electronic method.'],
    ['A blood bank assigns a group O D-negative unit to an emergency trauma patient in shock before typing.', 'Emergency release of O-negative red cells to an unknown patient is based on which blood group principle?', 'easy', 'C', 'O carries A and B antigens', 'O-negative cells have anti-A and anti-B', 'O-negative red cells lack A, B, and D antigens and are compatible with most recipients', 'O-negative serum lyses the recipient cells', 'O-negative red cells have no A, B, or D antigens, so they avoid the common ABO incompatibility in an emergency; the recipient antibodies in the plasma do not attack the donor cells. Transfusion of O cells is standard emergency policy until typing and crossmatch are complete.'],
    ['A 45-year-old woman with a history of a cold antibody has a transfusion request; her antibody screen is positive at 4 C only.', 'A cold antibody that is not clinically significant at 37 C is distinguished from a clinically significant cold antibody by which property?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Reactivity at 37 C with IAT', 'Reactivity strength, thermal range, and whether it reacts at 37 C', 'ABO type', 'Lack of any reactivity', 'Cold antibodies of the Lewis and I system usually react at low temperature and are clinically insignificant, while cold agglutinins with a 37 C thermal range (such as high-titer cold hemolysins) can cause hemolysis; the thermal amplitude and titer determine clinical relevance.'],
    ['A patient with an anti-K antibody has a history of a severe delayed reaction; the laboratory counsels the clinician about antigen avoidance.', 'Which red cell phenotype pair should be selected for a patient with anti-K?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'K positive cells', 'Random units', 'K-negative cells', 'D-negative cells only', 'A patient with anti-K must receive red cells lacking the K antigen (K-negative by antibody or antigen typing) to avoid an acute or delayed hemolytic reaction from the anamnestic antibody. Random units risk the 9% prevalence of K and could trigger the reaction.'],
    ['An antibody is identified as anti-Lewis; the blood bank verifies whether it is clinically significant.', 'Antibodies to Lewis (Le-a, Le-b) are clinically insignificant for transfusion because they are primarily which class and demonstrate what reactivity?', 'advanced', 'A', 'IgM antibodies reactive in saline at room temperature and not at 37 C', 'IgG antibodies that bind complement only', 'Complement-fixing antibodies with a 37 C range', 'IgG warm autoantibodies', 'Lewis antibodies are IgM and react best at room temperature or 4 C with saline techniques, are absorbed by the sial-acid destined, and are associated with a negative IAT, so antigen-negative units are not routinely required. They are not clinically significant at physiologic temperature.'],
    ['A cold antibody with a high titer causes in vivo hemolysis in a cold agglutinin disease patient; the patient needs a crossmatch.', 'The most reliable method to distinguish a high-titer cold agglutinin from a clinically significant warm antibody is which step?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Repeat the screen at 4 C', 'Pre-warm the tubes and cells to 37 C and test at 37 C with a pre-warm IAT', 'Add albumin to the test', 'Test with only A1 cells', 'Pre-warming eliminates the cold antibody that otherwise agglutinates at room temperature, revealing the underlying warm antibody reactivity; the pre-warm IAT is the standard procedure for cold autoantibodies and cold agglutinins. Testing at 4 C or with A1 cells intensifies the cold effect rather than clarifying the specificity.'],
    ['A patient develops acute intravascular hemolysis after an ABO-incompatible transfusion; the blood bank performs a clerical check.', 'The most likely cause of an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction is which finding on review?', 'easy', 'B', 'A weak D patient', 'ABO-incompatible unit issued by a clerical or identification error', 'A cold antibody with low titer', 'An alloanti-C that was missed', 'Most acute hemolytic reactions from ABO mismatch are avoidable clerical events (mislabeled tubes, wrong patient identification) that result in incompatible risk; tube labeling and patient identity check at the bedside are the primary preventive steps. Weak D and cold and C antibodies produce different reaction patterns or are less rapid.'],
    ['A patient develops fever, hypotension, and hemoglobinemia 30 minutes into a group A unit transfusion; the direct antiglobulin test is strongly positive.', 'The immune-mediated destruction in an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction is driven most directly by which mechanism?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'IgA complement only', 'Macrophage lysis without antibody', 'IgM anti-A or anti-B fixing complement causing rapid intravascular hemolysis', 'T-cell mediated lysis', 'ABO-incompatible plasma contains IgM class anti-A/anti-B that fix complement on the donor cells, producing the rapid intravascular hemolysis with hemoglobinemia, shock, and DIC; the strong DAT reflects the coated incompatible cells. Macrophages and T cells do not produce the syndrome rapidly without the complement-fixing antibody.'],
    ['A delayed hemolytic reaction is suspected in a patient 10 days after a transfusion; the hemoglobin falls with a positive DAT and a newly detected antibody.', 'The laboratory picture of a delayed hemolytic reaction shows which combination of findings?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Negative DAT with a negative screen', 'A positive DAT with a new alloantibody on the panel and a falling hemoglobin', 'Positive screen with only a cold antibody', 'A high reticulocyte count with no anemia', 'Delayed hemolysis follows anamnestic antibody production: the antibody was present at sub-detectable levels earlier, and after transfusion the titer rises, coating the transfused cells and producing a positive DAT and hemoglobin drop days later. The screen converts from negative to positive with the newly detectable specificity.'],
    ['A dim anesthesia patient develops hypotension during a platelet transfusion; the blood bank is asked to attribute the reaction.', 'A non-hemolytic febrile reaction during a platelet transfusion is most commonly caused by which component?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'Red cell antibodies', 'Allergic IgE', 'Cytokines released from the stored platelets (interleukins and complement) along with recipient antibodies', 'Plasma albumin', 'Febrile non-hemolytic reactions to platelets are largely cytokine-driven from the stored component, and leukoreduction lowers the risk; the reaction is not hemolytic and does not require stopping the ABO compatibility. IgE allergic reactions and red cell antibodies produce different clinical pictures.'],
    ['A patient develops urticaria and angioedema during a plasma transfusion; the reaction is classified.', 'An allergic (urticarial) reaction to a minor plasma protein is managed by which approach?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Stop and think nothing', 'Slow the transfusion and give antihistamines; the reaction is usually self-limited', 'Confirm a hemolytic episode', 'Recheck the crossmatch', 'Urticarial reactions result from recipient IgE against a plasma component, and they are usually managed by slowing the rate and antihistamines without discontinuing unless the reaction is severe or systemic; washed components may help if recurrent. They carry no hemolysis and do not require an ABO review.'],
    ['A patient under massive transfusion receives rapid red cells and develops the syndrome of hemolysis and infection; a sample runs cold.', 'The citrate in blood components binds calcium during massive transfusion and can produce which laboratory-clinical effect?', 'advanced', 'A', 'Hypocalcemia with a prolonged APTT and seizures or arrhythmia', 'Hypercalcemia with tetany', 'Low hemoglobin synthesis', 'Citrate elevation and no clinical change', 'Citrate anticoagulant chelates calcium as large volumes are transfused, lowering ionized calcium and potentially prolonging clotting tests and causing symptoms of hypocalcemia; calcium repletion is part of massive transfusion management. Hypercalcemia is not produced, and the other effects do not result from citrate.'],
    ['A blood bank receives a stat request for a 4-unit group-specific transfusion; the patient blood type is B.', 'A group B patient may receive which red cell products?', 'easy', 'A', 'Group B or group O red cells', 'Group A and B only', 'Group AB only', 'Only group A', 'A group B recipient can receive B or O (universal donor) red cells because the recipient plasma contains anti-A, so A cells would be incompatible; O cells lack A and B and are safe. AB and A units are excluded by the anti-A in the recipient.'],
    ['A 25-year-old woman with a negative antibody screen is typed as group A, Rh D positive; she received a unit of group A red cells an hour ago and now has a fever.', 'The first step in the workup of any acute transfusion reaction is which action?', 'easy', 'B', 'Wait for the repeat antibody screen', 'Immediately stop the transfusion and check the identification and clerical steps, then sample', 'Give more platelets', 'Cancel the DAT', 'All acute transfusion reactions begin with stopping the transfusion, verifying the patient identity and unit number, and collecting a post-transfusion specimen (CBC, DAT, tube check) to distinguish hemolytic from febrile or allergic causes. Continuation risks escalation of an ABO mismatch.'],
    ['A blood bank director reviews the storage time of platelets in a massive hemorrhage protocol.', 'Platelets are stored at what temperature and for what maximum duration in standard component practice?', 'easy', 'C', '4 C for 7 days', '22 C for 5 days with constant agitation and monitoring', '22 C for 5 to 7 days with continuous agitation', '0 C for 14 days', 'Platelet concentrates are stored at 22 degrees Celsius with continuous gentle agitation for 5 to 7 days depending on the bag, and they must be continually shaken to maintain the discoid shape and function; refrigeration activates and destroys them. Fresh frozen plasma is stored frozen and red cells 4 C for 42 days in additive solution.'],
    ['A patient receives cryoprecipitate for a fibrinogen of 60 mg/dL during massive transfusion.', 'Cryoprecipitate contains the highest concentration of which hemostatic proteins?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Factor VIII, von Willebrand factor, and fibrinogen with factor XIII', 'Platelets and factor II', 'Antithrombin and protein C', 'Factor V only', 'Cryoprecipitate is prepared by thawing frozen plasma and is rich in fibrinogen, factor VIII, von Willebrand factor, fibronectin, and factor XIII; it is used when the fibrinogen falls below 100 mg/dL and in von Willebrand disease or hemophilia A when concentrates are unavailable. It does not contain platelets or the natural anticoagulants at the dosing concentration.'],
    ['A patient with a low platelet count needs a platelet count increment check after a transfusion.', 'The corrected count increment (CCI) is calculated to evaluate a platelet transfusion response using which parameters?', 'advanced', 'C', 'Hemoglobin fall times', 'Only the pre-transfusion count', 'Post-count minus pre-count, the dose, and the body surface area', 'WBC and dose', 'The CCI = (post precondition count - pre count) x body surface area / number of platelets transfused; a low CCI indicates refractoriness from immune or non-immune causes. It standardizes the response across patient size and dose.'],
    ['A platelet-refractory patient has HLA antibodies; the blood bank is asked to select an appropriate product.', 'For a patient with anti-HLA antibodies causing platelet refractoriness, the recommended component is which type?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Random ABO platelets', 'HLA-matched or crossmatch-compatible platelets', 'Red cells', 'Cryoprecipitate', 'HLA alloimmunized patients destroy random platelets; providing HLA-matched or crossmatch-compatible apheresis platelets improves the increment, often with leukoreduction to prevent future immunization. ABO-matched random units would still be cleared by the reactive HLA antibodies.'],
    ['A sickle cell disease patient needs a chronic transfusion; the blood bank policy emphasizes extended phenotyping.', 'For a sickle cell patient on chronic transfusion, the best preventive strategy for alloimmunization is which approach?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'ABO-only matching', 'Group-specific only', 'Extended matching for Rh (C, c, E, e) and Kell antigens where feasible', 'Random units', 'Sickle patients form Rh and Kell antibodies at a high rate, so prophylactic extended matching reduces alloimmunization and delayed hemolysis; many centers match for C, E, and K antigens, especially in each transfusion program. ABO matching alone does not address the major risk antigens.'],
    ['An African American blood donor is typed as D positive but the cells are tested for the C and c functions to identify a partial D situation.', 'The R0 (Dce) haplotype and the absence of the C and E antigens are associated with which D variant?', 'advanced', 'D', 'Weak D type 1', 'The A2 subgroup', 'The Bombay phenotype', 'The most common partial D (sometimes D-negative typing) found in people of African descent, which risks anti-D formation when treated as D negative', 'Partial D variants such as DIIIa and others occur at higher frequency in African descent and may type as D negative with monoclonal reagents; an immunogenic partial D can make anti-D if the missing epitopes filter the challenge. The R0 pattern with C negative, E negative cells is the historical setting for these variants.'],
    ['A blood bank determines a patient to be D negative but the RHCE and DAg panel shows a partial D profile.', 'A D-negative designation for a partial D patient biases transfusion policy toward which approach?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Transfuse D positive always', 'Provide D-negative products so the patient cannot make anti-D against the D epitopes', 'Ignore the D and give any unit', 'Send the patient to the surgery ward', 'For partial D patients who lack part of the D mosaic, D-negative red cells avoid immunization against the missing D epitopes; the clinical transfusant should follow the status determined by molecular or serologic classification. Providing D-positive units risks anti-D formation and serious complications.'],
    ['A donor with O-positive cells fails a reverse group; the blood bank investigates a possible B(A) or weak B phenotype.', 'Detection of a weak B antigen that would otherwise be missed depends on which refinement of the typing?', 'advanced', 'C', 'Immediate spin only', 'Saline dilutions at 4 C', 'Sensitive reagent and incubated typing with low ionic strength enhancement', 'Capsule method', 'Weak B variants produce very weak reactivity that needs incubation at room temperature or 37 C with carefully selected anti-B reagents to be seen; the confirmation pair is the reverse grouping and the uptake of the retyped antisera. Immediate spin alone would miss the weak expression.'],
    ['A pregnant D-negative woman is screened for anti-D; the titer is 128 and there is a history of prior affected infant.', 'The management strategy for an RhD-sensitized pregnancy in the blood bank serial titer is which approach?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Serial anti-D titers with clinical correlation and monitoring for fetal anemia', 'Titer alone ignoring history', 'IgG quantification without titer', 'Only cord blood testing at delivery', 'An anti-D titers serial value that jumps by two or more dilutions, especially with a previously affected infant, prompts fetal monitoring for anemia and centric management, including middle cerebral artery Doppler and intrauterine transfusion planning. The history of a prior affected pregnancy is the most important predictor of severity.'],
    ['A postpartum RhD-negative mother with a group O D-positive infant requires the dose of RhIg to be calculated.', 'The fetomaternal hemorrhage (FMH) is quantitated by which laboratory method to determine the dose of RhIg needed?', 'advanced', 'B', 'Platelet count', 'The Kleihauer-Betke acid elution or fetal cell flow cytometry after the delivery', 'Hemoglobin A1c', 'Direct Coombs reagent', 'The Kleihauer-Betke test counts fetal red cells in the maternal circulation and normalizes the FMH volume; more than one standard dose of RhIg is needed when the fetal blood volume is large. Flow cytometry gives an objective alternative, and the other tests do not quantitate fetal cells.'],
    ['A 28-year-old woman with a threatened abortion at 10 weeks presents to the emergency blood bank; her type is O D-negative.', 'The indication for RhIg in this setting and the dose schedule follow which principle?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'No indication unless delivery', 'One dose at delivery only', 'RhIg within 72 hours of the potential sensitizing event to cover the FMH, with quantitation when a large bleed is suspected', 'RhIg at 20 weeks', 'RhIg is given after any potential sensitizing event (delivery, abortion, amniocentesis, abdominal trauma) within 72 hours; the dose is 300 ug for up to 30 mL of fetal whole blood, with KB quantitation for larger bleeds. Waiting until delivery would allow sensitization from the earlier event.'],
    ['A patient has an extra reactivity on an antibody panel that co-occurs with a cold antibody; the identification uses which technique?', 'A warm panagglutinin and a cold antibody in a complex autoantibody case are separated and identified using which method?', 'advanced', 'D', 'Test at 4 C', 'Dilute the serum 1:4', '>Room temperature saline', 'Autoadsorption (after pre-warming) and selected cell adsorption of autoantibody before panel testing', 'When a warm autoantibody masks all panel cells, autoadsorption removes the autoantibody and reveals underlying alloantibodies; the pre-warmed method separately handles cold antibodies. The adsorption is the key to clarify the specificity.'],
    ['A patient on a drug that causes drug-induced immune hemolysis has a positive DAT; the investigation pairs the clinical and laboratory picture.', 'Drug-induced immune hemolytic anemia (DIIHA) is confirmed by demonstrating which laboratory pattern?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Negative DAT despite hemolysis', 'A positive DAT with a drug-dependent antibody demonstrable only in the presence of the drug', 'An anti-A titer', 'A negative eluate', 'DIIHA antibodies react only in the presence of the offending drug, so the DAT is positive and the eluate or serum reacts with cells only when the drug is added; the pattern differentiates drug-dependent (hapten and immune complex) from drug-induced autoantibody. A negative DAT or a nonreactive eluate would not fit the mechanism.'],
    ['A patient is receiving granulocytes for a severe neutropenic infection; the blood bank issues the component.', 'Granulocyte concentrates are stored under which condition and for what limited shelf life?', 'advanced', 'C', 'Refrigerated 4 C for 3 days', 'Room temperature for 3 days without agitation', 'Stored at 22 C with agitation for up to 24 hours after apheresis', 'Frozen 7 days', 'Granulocytes are collected by apheresis and must be transfused within 24 hours, stored at room temperature with gentle agitation because granulocytes are labile and activated by cold and time. The short shelf life reflects the rapid functional decline of the granulocyte.'],
    ['A patient with IgA deficiency develops anaphylaxis during a plasma transfusion.', 'Severe anaphylactic reactions to IgA occur in patients with which condition and are best prevented by which component?', 'advanced', 'A', 'Anti-IgA antibodies in an IgA deficient patient, using washed components or an IgA-deficient donor', 'ABO incompatibility', 'Keloid reactivity', 'IgM autoantibody', 'A patient with IgA deficiency may form anti-IgA and anaphylactically react to IgA in the transfused product; washed red cells or components from IgA-deficient donors avoid the shock. ABO mismatch and cold agglutinins produce different clinical syndromes and do not depend on IgA concentration.'],
    ['A blood bank performs quality testing on a current platelet unit and the pH limit is assessed.', 'The pH of the platelet product during storage is monitored and must stay above which threshold to maintain platelet viability?', 'advanced', 'B', 'Higher than 5.0 at the end of storage', 'Higher than 6.2 to 6.4 during most of storage (with 6.0 to 6.4 at the expiry)', 'Higher than 7.0 always', 'Higher than 8.0', 'Platelet pH falls as metabolism releases lactate; the requirement is that pH remains above about 6.2 in most samples and not below 6.0 at the end of storage, with per-donor units released accordingly. Values below this indicate that platelets have undergone the storage lesion and are not clinically effective.'],
    ['A blood bank transmits a patient specimen for a group O, RhD-negative emergency and a second sample arrives for confirmation.', 'The correct ABO and Rh confirmation strategy for a new patient needs which sample requirement?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'One tube only', 'Two draws from separate collection tubes is standard to confirm the type before transfusion', 'A single draw with duplicate analysis', 'Historical typing', 'For transfusion therapy, ABO/Rh is confirmed by two separately collected specimens (or one acceptable system) so a labeling error cannot result in an incompatible release; the current practice requires two determinations on the record. This is the same two-Tube principle that the electronic crossmatch relies on.'],
    ['A neonate requires a top-up transfusion for anemia; the blood bank is asked about the compatibility rules.', 'For a neonatal top-up transfusion, the mother serum and the neonate serum are tested against which units?', 'advanced', 'D', 'No test needed', 'Only ABO forward', 'Crossmatch against the father', 'A crossmatch against the prospective units using neonatal or maternal serum, with selection of cytomegalovirus-negative or leukoreduced red cells as policy', 'In neonates, the infant makes little antibody, so the maternal serum (which may contain the immunoglobulin of concern) and the neonatal serum are used for the crossmatch, and the units are usually CMV-seronegative or leukoreduced plus sickle-negative as per policy. The father type is not involved in the compatibility test.'],
    ['A group AB patient has a massive bleed and the blood bank notifies the physician about the availability of group AB units.', 'A group AB patient can receive which red cell type, and why?', 'easy', 'A', 'AB, A, B, or O red cells, since the patient has no clinically significant plasma ABO antibodies', 'Only AB', 'Only O', 'Only B', 'An AB recipient has no anti-A or anti-B in plasma, so A, B, and O red cells are compatible as well as AB; plasma products for an AB patient must be compatible with the anti-recipient, but red cells are widely accepted. This is the universal recipient pattern.'],
    ['A newborn with moderate jaundice and a positive DAT is evaluated for ABO HDN; the mother is group O.', 'The mild severity of ABO hemolytic disease, with a higher-than-expected direct antiglobulin test positivity but low need for exchange, reflects which feature of the mechanism?', 'advanced', 'B', 'The antibody is IgM', 'A/B antigens are weakly developed on neonatal red cells and the anti-A or anti-B is usually low titer IgG1 with limited transplacental passage', 'The complement is always activated', 'IgA crosses the placenta', 'Neonatal A and B antigens are weak, the offending maternal antibody is generally of a low titer, and ABO HDN is generally mild; exchange transfusion is rarely required, and the problem is managed by phototherapy. The strength of the DAT does not predict the modest clinical severity in the ABO form.'],
    ['An anti-cell unidentified specificity is masked by the autologous; the technologist uses a phenotype panel of rare cells to understand the antibody.', 'When a patient has an antibody to a high-frequency antigen, the transfusion approach is which?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'Provide any random unit', 'Provide the antigen the patient reacted to', 'Identify the specificity, then find antigen-negative units or use the patient as a self-donor for emergent needs', 'Refuse the crossmatch', 'High-frequency antigen antibodies (anti-k, anti-Kpb, anti-En-a) make obtaining compatible units difficult; after identification, an antigen-negative or autologous unit is arranged, and the blood bank searches rare donor registries. Random units with the antigen would react and risk hemolysis.'],
    ['A patient is found to have anti-e, and the blood bank reviews the phenotype of the donor units.', 'For a patient with anti-e, which donor cells can be used?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Any e-positive', 'e-negative cells selected after the anti-e is confirmed', 'Only E positive', 'Only D positive', 'Anti-e requires e-negative donor cells; since e is an Rh antigen with high frequency, the search uses e-negative units which may need rare types and donor registry cooperation. The antibody was confirmed by the panel when the pack reaction pattern fit the anti-e image.'],
    ['A patient with septicemia is transfused and develops a high fever within 15 minutes of the start; a platelet product is suspected of bacterial contamination.', 'The risk of bacterial contamination in platelet products is mitigated by which pretransfusion step?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Bacterial culture and pH detection with rapid testing as well as reduced storage time and appropriate temperature', 'Sterile filtration of all products', 'Adding antibiotics to the unit', 'Gamma irradiation only', 'Platelet bacterial contamination is controlled by bacterial screening (culture), pH measurement, reduced storage out to 5 to 7 days, and risk-based testing algorithms; irradiation prevents lymphocyte proliferation rather than bacterial contamination. The aerobic storage at room temperature creates the contamination risk.'],
    ['A blood bank processes a unit for a patient with IgA deficiency, washing the red cells.', 'Washing red cells removes which components that are most relevant for an IgA-deficient patient?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'The red cells themselves', 'The potassium', 'Plasma proteins including IgA and most leukocytes', 'The sodium citrate', 'Washing red cells removes most of the residual plasma including immunoglobulins and most leukocytes, so an IgA-deficient patient avoids the plasma IgA that causes anaphylaxis. Potassium and citrate are only partially reduced, and the red cells themselves remain.'],
    ['A patient with a bone marrow transplant has an ABO incompatibility; the blood bank manages the mixed red cell population.', 'ABO-mismatched stem cell transplantation requires selection of the appropriate blood group during the peri-transplant period, tracked by which monitoring?', 'advanced', 'D', 'Only the pre-transplant type', 'Hemoglobin only', 'Mean platelet volume', 'Serial ABO typing of the patient during engraftment to follow the change from recipient to donor type', 'ABO-mismatched SCT requires following the red cell type and the isoagglutinin titer as the marrow engrafts, so the blood bank switches support from the recipient type to the donor type and tracks mixed-field typing. The phase of conversion determines which compatible product is used for red cells and plasma.'],
    ['A patient needs a crossmatch with a sample from a low-count platelet but the request is stat for surgery.', 'Which crossmatch method is acceptable when the antibody screen is negative and there is no history of antibodies, and why?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Full AHG on every unit', 'Electronic or immediate spin after two-type verification, because a negative screen makes AHG-crossmatch unnecessary', 'A four antigen panel', 'No crossmatch at all', 'With a negative antibody screen and confirmed ABO/Rh, an electronic crossmatch (or immediate spin) is sufficient because there is no antibody to detect with the antiglobulin method; the AHG crossmatch adds no value in the absence of a positive screen. This is the basis of computer crossmatching.'],
    ['A blood bank is asked to type a donor with a suspected A2B phenotype and to resolve the subgroup.', 'An A2B donor has which typing pattern on the reverse (serum) side?', 'advanced', 'C', 'Anti-B only', 'Anti-A only', 'Occasionally a cold anti-A1 in the serum along with the expected anti-A negative pattern', 'No antibodies', 'A2B is the ABO subgroup in which the A antigen is weak; rare A2B individuals may carry a naturally occurring anti-A1 that reacts only at cold temperatures, making the reverse-type result ambiguous. The presence of the cold anti-A1 is the discordant marker, detected only at 4 C with A1 cells.'],
    ['A patient has mycoplasma pneumonia and a high-titer cold agglutinin; the blood bank must manage the compatibility procedures.', 'A high-titer cold agglutinin that interferes with the antibody screen responds best to which of these procedures?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Pre-warming and testing at 37 C with a pre-warm technique', 'Refrigeration for 4 hours', 'Adding complement', 'Adding red cells only', 'The pre-warm technique keeps all reagents at 37 C so the cold agglutinin cannot agglutinate the cells; the sample and panels are warmed before mixing, and testing at 37 C separates the autologous cold reactivity from any warm antibody. Cold amplification and complement do not remove the interference.'],
    ['A patient has a weak ABO reaction that persists; the technologist decides to confirm the subgroup with an adsorption-elution technique.', 'Adsorption-elution confirms weak A/B subgroups by using which principle?', 'advanced', 'B', 'Adding enzyme', 'Concentrating the antigen by adsorption of antibody onto the cells followed by elution and testing the eluate', 'Use of a dye', 'Repeated freezing', 'In adsorption-elution, test cells coated with antibody are washed and the antibody eluted, then tested against group A and B cells; the recovered antibody proves the presence of the weak antigen substantiated by the eluate. Freezing, enzymes, and dyes do not elicit the antigen-antibody relation in this way.'],
    ['An apheresis platelet donor develops a risk of TRALI; the blood bank reviews donor eligibility policies.', 'The leading prevention strategy for transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) is to avoid which component?', 'advanced', 'C', 'Red cells with high potassium', 'Cold stored components', 'High-plasma components from female donors with a history of pregnancy or antibodies (male-predominant or antibody-screened plasma)', 'Age of the components', 'TRALI results from donor antibodies (often HLA class II or HNA) reacting with the recipient leukocytes; screening donors, especially parous females, and using male-predominant or antibody-negative plasma reduces the risk. Potassium, cold storage, and component age are not the primary TRALI determinants.'],
    ['A patient receives a unit that has not been irradiated; the blood bank confirms that irradiation is intended for which recipients.', 'Gamma irradiation of cellular components is indicated for which recipient group?', 'easy', 'B', 'All patients with normal immunity', 'Immunocompromised and at-risk patients including bone marrow transplant, intrauterine transfusion, and directed donation from a blood relative', 'Only the elderly', 'Only ABO-compatible patients', 'Irradiation prevents transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease by inactivating the donor lymphocytes; units are irradiated for immunocompromised patients and for directed donations from blood relatives. Normal immune hosts do not require the treatment.'],
    ['A blood bank must issue a unit to a neonatal exchange patient whose mother has anti-c.', 'For an exchange transfusion in a neonate whose mother has a clinically significant antibody, the red cells should be which phenotype?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'Any compatible by ABO', 'c positive', 'Antigen-negative for the maternal antibody, CMV-seronegative or leukoreduced, and ABO/Rh compatible with the maternal plasma', 'D positive only', 'The neonate inherits the paternal antigen; for exchange, the units must be antigen negative for the maternal antibody that has crossed the placenta, CMV-safe, and compatible with the maternal serum. ABO compatibility alone does not cover the clinically significant antibody.'],
    ['A blood center collects components from a donor and tests the sample for infectious disease markers.', 'The infectious disease testing requirements for donated blood include which combination of markers?', 'intermediate', 'D', 'Only syphilis', 'Only HbA1c', 'Cholesterol panel', 'HIV, hepatitis B and C, syphilis and additional regional markers (HTLV, West Nile, Chagas) depending on local policy and testing systems', 'Blood donors are screened for the transfusion-transmitted infections with nucleic acid and serologic testing covering HIV, HBV, HCV, syphilis, and additional agents per policy; the specific combination follows the national regulations. The other options omit the required viremia markers.'],
    ['A blood bank records the expiry of an AS-1 red cell unit.', 'Additive solution red cell units (AS-1, AS-3, AS-5) stored at 1 to 6 C have which shelf life?', 'easy', 'A', '42 days', '21 days', '5 days', '14 days', 'Red cells in an additive solution such as AS-1 with acceptable hematocrit have a 42-day shelf life at 1 to 6 C, versus 21 days for CPD and 35 days for CPDA-1; the additive solution maintains ATP and viability. Platelet and plasma storage times are distinct, and the count, not the additive, is unrelated to the 5-day platelet shelf life.'],
    ['A patient develops a rising bilirubin 5 to 10 days after a transfusion and the antibody screen is now positive for anti-Jka.', 'The delayed hemolytic reaction that develops days after transfusion is best avoided in future transfusions by which practice?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Using only fresh units', 'Serial follow-up and extended matching supported by the historical record so the evanescent antibody is not missed', 'Relying on a single immediate mix', 'Providing ABO-only units', 'Because Kidd antibodies drop below the detection threshold, the historical finding of the antibody prevents its anamnestic recurrence; robust records and extended antigen typing mitigate the delayed reaction. Fresh units and immediate mixing do not address the evanescent character.'],

];
